    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> invaded on 7 December 1975, nine days after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fretilin">Fretilin</a>'s declaration of independence; the occupation that followed caused at least 102,800 conflict-related deaths between 1974 and 1999, by one estimate as many as 200,000.</li><li>A UN-supervised referendum in August 1999 voted clearly for independence, met by a punitive campaign of violence by pro-integration militias backed by elements of the Indonesian military, before UNTAET took over the territory on 25 October 1999.</li><li>Timor-Leste became independent on 20 May 2002; its Petroleum Fund, set up in 2005, received $23 billion from oil sales by 2021 and funds 80% of government spending, but is at risk of exhaustion as the Bayu-Undan field runs out.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Timor-Leste's name means 'East East': <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timor">Timor</a> comes from timur, 'east' in Indonesian or Malay, and Leste is 'east' in Portuguese. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetum-language">Tetun</a> the country is Timór Lorosa'e, 'where the sun rises'.</p></aside>
    </section>

